Financing mechanisms should be evaluated as part of a financing strategy for conservation and restoration programs. A financial assessment considers the project’s scope, spatial scale, strategic activities and time frame, as well as total costs, current sources of revenue, and gaps. A sustainable financing strategy evaluates the total funding available from all sources — government budgets; funding from private donors, corporate or NGO partners; revenue generated by access and user fees, fines, and other payment schemes. The assessment estimates the funding needed and determines the financing gap that must be filled to meet the program’s conservation or restoration goals. A financial assessment then evaluates the legal, administrative, social, political, and environmental context to determine the most appropriate finance mechanisms (see the Guide to Conservation Finance).
